  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-12a159d0 Web logs overview\"**\n- Four panels: Total requests (count), Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`), Requests over time (date histogram on `timestamp`), and Responses by status code (pie on `response.keyword`).\n- Time range is set to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31. The sample data runs from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, which extends past today (2026-10-12), so a default \"last 7 days\" view would show nothing.\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-12a159d0-web-logs-overview-743d9e0d71f1\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-12a159d0 5xx spike\"**\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Query matches `response.keyword` with the wildcard `5*`, so every 5xx code counts.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window, checked every 1 minute.\n- Enabled, with ID `56914617-ce26-4e5a-aa5e-23ed227f36d1`. Its status is still `pending` because it has only just started.\n\n**Things to know**\n- The data contains only 200, 404 and 503 responses, so the 5xx condition is effectively `response = 503`. The rule will fire only if 5xx traffic appears in the last 5 minutes. Since the sample data is historical, it may never fire unless new 5xx events are indexed.\n- The rule has no action connector, so it records alerts but sends no notifications.\n- `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` isn't set in my shell. I used the Kibana MCP tools, which are connected, instead of calling the REST API directly.",
